"Figure caption: Lipid nanoparticles loaded with nucleic acid therapeutics must overcome numerous biological barriers to achieve effective in vivo gene regulationCreated with BioRender.com.\n\nFigure caption: Depiction of LNP structure and surface modificationsLNPs typically include the RNA cargo along with helper lipids, ionizable lipids, cholesterol, and PEG-lipids. To enable cell-specific targeting, the surface of LNPs can be modified with antibodies, peptides, aptamers, or proteins.Created with BioRender.com.\n\nFigure caption: Depiction of the composition of membrane-modified LNPsRNA-loaded LNPs and liposomes have been coated with membranes derived from macrophages, cancer cells, lymphocytes, CHO cells, and the endoplasmic reticulum of cancer cells to enable improved biointerfacing and enhanced gene regulation in target cells. Created with BioRender.com.\n\nFigure caption: Characterization and anti-cancer efficacy of LNPs loaded with siADAR1 and coated with CHO membranes engineered to express PD1(A) Scheme of siADAR1-LNP@mPD1 preparation and the principle of antitumor immune response. (B) TEM images of uncoated and membrane-modified LNPs. (C) SDS-PAGE and western blot of I: siADA1-LNPs, II: mPD1 membrane nanovesicles, and III: siADAR1-LNP@mPD1. (D) Therapeutic timeline for study evaluating treatment of subcutaneous 4T1 tumors. (E) Average tumor growth kinetics in mice after treatment with each group indicated in the legend (data are mean ± standard deviation, n = 10). (F) The RT-qPCR analysis of the relative expression of ADAR1 mRNA from excised 4T1 tumors cells after different treatments (data are mean ± standard deviation, n = 3). Reproduced with permission from Ding et al.61\n\nFigure caption: Preparation and in vitro evaluation of membrane-modified, siRNA-loaded LNPs for HIV therapy(A) Illustration of the synthesis of lymphocyte membrane- and 12p1-dual functionalized siRNA-loaded lipid nanoparticles (MPLN). (B) Proposed mechanism of action of MPLN. (C) Inhibition of various treatments on X4-tropic HIV-1NL4-3 infection. LN, lipid nanoparticle; MLN, membrane-modified lipid nanoparticle; MPLN, membrane- and 12p1-modified lipid nanoparticle. Blocked samples were treated with antibody mixtures of CD4, CXCR4, and CCR5 (2:2:1, W/W/W) to confirm the possible binding site. Data were presented as mean ± SD, n = 3, p values calculated using the two-sample t test. a: p < 0.05 versus control; b: p < 0.05 versus free siRNA; c: p < 0.05 versus LN; d: p < 0.05 versus MLN; e: p < 0.05 versus antibody mixtures; f: p < 0.05 versus MPLN; g: p < 0.05 versus scrambled siRNA/MPLN; and h: p < 0.05 versus antibody blocked MLN. (D) Levels of tat and rev mRNA in HIV-1NL4-3-infected cells treated with LN, MLN, MPLN, scrambled siRNA/MPLN, antibody-blocked MLN, and antibody-blocked MPLN. The level of mRNA was analyzed on a real-time fluorescence quantitative PCR detection system. SYBR Green was used. Data were presented as mean ± SD, n = 3, p values were calculated using the two-sample t test. a: p < 0.05 versus control; b: p < 0.05 versus free siRNA; c: p < 0.05 versus LN; d: p < 0.05 versus MLN; e: p < 0.05 versus MPLN; f: p < 0.05 versus scrambled siRNA/MPLN; and g: p < 0.05 versus antibody-blocked MLN. Reproduced with permission from Zhang et al.65\n\nFigure caption: Intracellular trafficking pathways and in vitro effect of PEhCv/siRNA NPs in U87 cells(A) Visual representation of colocalization at various time points with lysosomes, Golgi, and endoplasmic reticulum. Colocalization is denoted by yellow puncta, which signify the merging of fluorescently marked cellular components (green) with Cy5-tagged siRNA (red). Scale bars, 25 μm. (B) Quantitative analysis of colocalization ratios at different times. The colocalization ratios were calculated based on the Pearson’s correlation coefficient from multiple images (n = 3) with a normalized calculational method. (C) Schematic illustration of intracellular trafficking pathways of PEhCv/siRNA NPs. (D) and (E) Quantification of EGFR protein levels in U87 cells via western blot analysis. (F) Evaluation of the toxicological effects of diverse NPs on U87 cells (n = 6). ∗∗p < 0.01 and ∗∗∗p < 0.001, following ANOVA. Reproduced with permission from Tu et al.66\n\nTable caption: Summary of membrane-modified LNPs and liposomes that have been developed for RNA delivery"